{
  "answer_markdown": "To keep one agent and update it instead of creating a new one on every run, set an explicit `id` on your `Agent` instance before calling `Runner.run()`. This causes the workflow to call `beta.agents.update()` on the existing agent rather than creating a new one. If you omit `id`, a new remote agent is created each time [1].\n\nExample:\n\n```python\nagent = workflows_mistralai.Agent(\n    id=\"ag_abc123\",  # Reuse an existing remote agent\n    model=\"mistral-medium-latest\",\n    name=\"finance-agent\",\n    instructions=\"Use tools to answer financial questions.\",\n    tools=[get_interest_rate],\n)\n```",
  "citations": [
    {
      "n": 1,
      "quote": "When a `RemoteSession` initializes a conversation, it iterates over every agent in the handoff graph and either creates or updates it via the Agents API:"
    },
    {
      "n": 1,
      "quote": "**`Agent(id=None)`**: a new remote agent is created with `beta.agents.create()`. The returned ID is stored back on the `Agent` object, so subsequent runs with the **same instance** reuse it."
    },
    {
      "n": 1,
      "quote": "**`Agent(id=\"existing-id\")`**: the existing remote agent is updated with `beta.agents.update()`. No new agent is created."
